{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpmQKx6c7BarRt1SE4E5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worth separating two things, because I think they pull in different directions.\n\nFor the slugify case specifically, a second model wouldn't have helped. The bottleneck wasn't reasoning capability — it was that the issue doesn't say what the slugs are *for*. A reviewer model reads the same four lines of ISSUE.md and has to guess from the same information I did, then states its guess confidently. Two models landing on ASCII-only isn't corroboration; it's correlated priors about what \"slug\" conventionally means. What actually resolves it is knowing whether these become URL paths, dedup keys, or filenames — and that lives in your product, not in another model.\n\nWhere outside review does earn its cost is when the verifier has an easier job than the generator: bug hunting, spec conformance, security review, \"does this actually do what the test claims.\" There's a ground truth to check against. Policy choices on underspecified edge cases have no ground truth, so review degrades into a second opinion that's hard to adjudicate — and it can quietly diffuse accountability, where \"the reviewer agreed\" substitutes for the call being right.\n\nThe cheap version of what you're after is roughly what I did here: make the call, pin the behavior in a named test, and flag it in the summary. That routes the decision to the human who has the missing context, at near-zero latency cost. Second-model review on top of that mostly adds confidence, not accuracy.\n\nI haven't acted on any of this — no agent spawned, nothing recorded. You said \"someday\" and \"might be,\" and I'd rather not convert a musing into a standing practice on my own. If you do want it as a real workflow rule, tell me the trigger you want (every ambiguous spec? only on public API surface? only before merge?) and I'll write it down properly."}],"containe